feedback.message:
PIXEL PAINTER - SECOND FEEDBACK (post-repair review)

The redo fix and resize content-preservation fix look correct. However, I found an additional defect:

=== DEFECT 3: Touch handling on canvas has no touchcancel listener ===
The app registers touchstart/touchmove/touchend but does NOT register a "touchcancel" handler. If the browser cancels the touch (e.g., due to a modal alert, system intervention, or scrolling trigger), the `painting` flag stays true and pointer events on subsequent touches would be mishandled. This is a low-probability but real defect for mobile/touch users.

=== DEFECT 4: initCanvas() resize content preservation has incorrect intermediate canvas sizing ===
In initCanvas(c, r, keep) when keep=true:
  const prev = document.createElement("canvas");
  prev.width = cols;   // BUG: cols is already set to NEW size c!
  prev.height = rows;  // same issue — should be OLD cols/rows
  prev.getContext("2d").putImageData(imageData, 0, 0);

The imageData buffer has the OLD canvas dimensions (the old cols×rows that existed before initCanvas was called). But `cols` and `rows` have ALREADY been overwritten to the NEW values at the top of the function. So prev is sized to NEW dimensions but gets an ImageData that's the OLD size. 

When imageData.data.length = oldCols * oldRows * 4, but prev.width * prev.height * 4 = newCols * newRows * 4, there are two sub-issues:
1) If old dimensions differ from new dimensions, putImageData may fail or silently truncate/corrupt because the source ImageData doesn't match the destination canvas size.
2) Even if it "works", the subsequent drawImage to temp (which is also sized to NEW dims) draws from an incorrectly-sized source canvas.

The fix should save old dimensions before overwriting:
  const oldCols = cols, oldRows = rows;
  cols = c; rows = r;
  ...
  if (keep && imageData) {
    const prev = document.createElement("canvas");
    prev.width = oldCols;   // use old dims!
    prev.height = oldRows;
    prev.getContext("2d").putImageData(imageData, 0, 0);
    const temp = document.createElement("canvas");
    temp.width = cols;
    temp.height = rows;
    ...

This is a real bug: resize content preservation silently produces incorrect/wrong results when old and new dimensions differ.

=== DEFECT 5 (potential): Right-click eraser via contextmenu only prevents default ===
The code has `canvas.addEventListener("contextmenu", (e) => e.preventDefault())` but never wires up right-button mouse events to the eraser. The hint says "Right-click drag erases" but there is no handler for mousedown/mousemove/mouseup on right-click (button=2). When user right-clicks, contextmenu is prevented (good) but pointerDown won't fire because... actually, it should still fire since contextmenu prevention doesn't cancel mousedown. Let me re-check: The mousedown handler has `if (e.type === "contextmenu") return;` — but that only returns for the contextmenu event itself, not for mouse events. However, pointerDown does NOT check e.button or right-click. So right-click mousedown WILL trigger painting with whatever tool is active (brush, not eraser). The hint says "Right-click drag erases" but there is no mechanism to switch to eraser on right-click.

FIX: Either add `e.button === 2` handling in pointerDown to use eraser temporarily, or update the hint text.

=== SUMMARY ===
Three additional defects to address:
1. Missing touchcancel handler (minor)
2. initCanvas resize content preservation uses wrong canvas dimensions (substantive — content gets garbled when resizing)  
3. Right-click eraser feature described in hint but not implemented
